Turn the password manager off on HarmonyOS until it can be trusted.

Two pieces are missing and both are load-bearing.

Login Data's passwords are encrypted through OSCrypt, and the only key
provider this platform registers is PosixKeyProvider, whose key is a constant
compiled into every Chromium build ("peanuts"/"saltysalt"). The file is
obfuscated, not protected: anyone who copies it can decrypt it offline,
without the device.

Nothing re-authenticates the user either. device_reauth has no OHOS
DeviceAuthenticator, so chrome://password-manager/ shows a stored password in
plaintext on a tap, and a saved password fills with no check at all.

IsFillingEnabled is the one place both saving and filling pass through --
IsSavingAndFillingEnabled calls it -- so one guard closes both. Passwords
already stored are left where they are; clearing browsing data removes them.

Revert this together with the HUKS key provider and the OHOS
DeviceAuthenticator, and add a startup migration that re-encrypts what the old
key wrote.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc b/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc
@@ -294,6 +294,23 @@
 bool ChromePasswordManagerClient::IsFillingEnabled(
     const url::Origin& origin,
     base::optional_ref<const GURL> url) const {
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // Temporary, and deliberately at the choke point both saving and filling
+  // pass through -- IsSavingAndFillingEnabled calls this.
+  //
+  // Two things are missing. Login Data's passwords go through OSCrypt, and
+  // the only key provider registered on this platform is PosixKeyProvider,
+  // whose key is a constant compiled into every Chromium build: the file is
+  // obfuscated, not protected, and anyone holding it can read it offline.
+  // Nothing re-authenticates the user before a stored password is shown or
+  // filled either, so the password manager page hands out plaintext on a tap.
+  //
+  // Storing more passwords under those terms only grows the exposure, so the
+  // manager is off until OSCrypt has a HUKS-backed key provider and
+  // DeviceAuthenticator has an OHOS implementation. Passwords already saved
+  // are left alone; clearing browsing data removes them.
+  return false;
+#else
   if (origin.opaque() &&
       base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(
           password_manager::features::kPasswordBlockOpaqueOrigins)) {
diff --git a/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc b/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/password_manager/chrome_password_manager_client.cc
@@ -343,6 +343,7 @@
   }
   return !ssl_errors &&
          IsPasswordManagementEnabledForCurrentPage(origin.GetURL());
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
 }
 
